# Beam SSH setup
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client (the machine you ssh FROM)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Put beam-client on your PATH.
# 2. Load the ssh wrapper from your shell rc:
#      bash (~/.bashrc):   eval "$(beam-client shell-init --shell bash)"
#      zsh  (~/.zshrc):    eval "$(beam-client shell-init --shell zsh)"
#      fish (config.fish): beam-client shell-init --shell fish | source
#
# `ssh host` then runs `beam-client ssh -- host`, which starts a listener on
# 127.0.0.1, and runs the real ssh with:
#      -o SendEnv=BEAM_SESSION_ID -o SendEnv=BEAM_LISTENER_PORT
#      -R <remote port>:127.0.0.1:<listener port>
# so nothing has to be added to ~/.ssh/config for Beam to work.
# Set BEAM_DISABLE=1 to bypass the wrapper; `ssh -O ...`, `-V`, `-G`, `-Q`
# always go straight to ssh.
#
# Optional, only if you also want plain `command ssh` sessions to forward the
# variables (they will not have a listener behind them, so this is rarely
# useful):
# Host *
#     SendEnv BEAM_SESSION_ID BEAM_LISTENER_PORT
#
# ControlMaster / multiplexing:
# Beam works with ControlMaster=auto: the extra -R is requested through the
# existing master and the variables are sent with the new session. Things to
# know:
#   - Each `ssh` run gets its own session ID and listener, so multiplexed
#     sessions do not share approvals.
#   - With ControlPersist, the reverse forward of a finished session stays
#     open on the server until the master exits (it points at a listener that
#     is gone; requests through it fail with "cannot reach the client").
#   - If you would rather avoid that, disable multiplexing for Beam hosts:
# Host my-beam-host
#     ControlMaster no
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server (the machine you ssh TO)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /etc/ssh/sshd_config (then reload sshd):
#     AcceptEnv BEAM_SESSION_ID BEAM_LISTENER_PORT
#     AllowTcpForwarding remote        # or "yes"; Beam needs remote (-R) forwards
#     # GatewayPorts must stay "no" (the default) so the forward binds to
#     # loopback only. PermitOpen does not apply to -R forwards; PermitListen
#     # (OpenSSH 7.8+) does, and its default ("any") is what Beam needs.
#
# Put beam-server-handler on PATH and load the command-not-found hook from
# the shell rc of the account you log in as:
#     bash (~/.bashrc):   eval "$(beam-server-handler shell-init --shell bash)"
#     zsh  (~/.zshrc):    eval "$(beam-server-handler shell-init --shell zsh)"
#     fish (config.fish): beam-server-handler shell-init --shell fish | source
# or append it once with:  beam-server-handler install --shell bash
#
# Note for bash: `ssh host 'cmd'` runs a non-interactive shell. Some builds
# (Debian/Ubuntu among them) still read ~/.bashrc for commands run over ssh,
# but their default .bashrc returns early when not interactive, so put the
# hook above that check if you want it for one-off commands too. Interactive
# logins are covered either way.
